10. Mistakes I see students make again and again

• Tutorial loop. Watching course after course without building. Rule: for every hour of video, spend at
least two hours writing your own code.
• Learning too many things. Django, Flask, FastAPI, React, Java and Flutter at the same time. Stick
to the stack in this guide until you are placed.
• Copying projects. Cloning a project from YouTube and putting it on your resume. Interviewers can
tell within three questions.
• Leaving DSA for later. DSA cannot be crammed in the last month. Start today, even with one easy
problem.
• Ignoring aptitude. Strong coders get filtered out in the first online test. Do not let that be you.
• Unfinished projects. Three finished and deployed projects beat eight unfinished ones every time.
• Comparing with others. Someone will always seem ahead. Compare yourself only with who you
were last month.
• Relying on AI to write your code. Use AI tools to understand concepts and debug, but write the
code yourself. If you cannot explain a line, you do not own it yet

11. Final checklist before placement season

Go through this list about one month before drives begin. Every item should honestly be a yes.

| Area | Ready when |
| :--- | :--- |
| DSA | 250 or more problems solved, and you can solve a medium level problem in about 30 minutes |
| SQL | Comfortable with joins, GROUP BY, subqueries and window functions |
| Python | Can explain OOP concepts with your own code examples |
| FastAPI | Can build a CRUD API with database and JWT authentication from scratch without a tutorial |
| Projects | At least four complete projects, at least three deployed, all with proper READMEs |
| GitHub | Profile README done, best repos pinned, consistent activity over months |
| Core CS | Short notes for OOP, DBMS, OS and CN revised at least twice |
| Aptitude | Scoring well and consistently on PrepInsta or IndiaBix mock tests |
| Resume | One page, reviewed by a senior or teacher, every line backed by real work |
| LinkedIn | Complete profile, a few posts about your projects |
| Interviews | At least five mock interviews done, project explanations practised out loud |

A last word
Placement is not a single exam that you pass or fail. It is the result of many small days of work added
together. If you follow this guide even seventy percent of the way, you will be ahead of most students in
your batch. Start today with something small: create your GitHub account, push your first file, and solve
one easy problem. Then do it again tomorrow.
All the best. Keep building.
